From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Build from a fresh clone

git-info.h is generated by make-git-info and is not in the repository,
and the Makefile declares the dependency on it for two object files out
of seventeen.  Every one of them needs it -- it is included by
macro11.h, which nearly every source includes -- and the dependency
files need it before anything else, since make remakes those first and
generating one runs the compiler.

So a build from a fresh clone stops with "git-info.h: No such file or
directory", six times over, before the header has been generated.  The
comment above those two lines ("which otherwise (sometime) gets
generated too late") says the author met this and fixed half of it.

Seen with GNU make 3.81, which is what macOS ships.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Makefile | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 1ed64a6..d5891be 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -46,8 +46,14 @@ git-info.h:
 
 # Bootstrap dependency on the git header file, which otherwise
 # (sometime) gets generated too late.
-macro11.o: git-info.h
-dumpobj.o: git-info.h
+#
+# Every object needs it, not just these two: it is included by macro11.h,
+# which nearly every source includes.  The dependency files need it
+# first of all -- make remakes those before anything else, and generating
+# one runs the compiler, which stops on the missing header.  Without them
+# in here a build from a fresh clone fails outright, since the header is
+# generated and not in the repository.
+$(ALL_SRCS:.c=.d) $(MACRO11_OBJS) $(DUMPOBJ_OBJS): git-info.h
 
 readme.lst: macro11 README.MAC
 	./macro11 README.MAC -l readme.lst -ysl 8 -e BMK
